---
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { boundaries, sensingBoundaries } from '../../data/boundaries';
import { CRATE_URL } from '../../consts';
import { numberWord } from '../../data/evidence';

const title = 'Boundaries | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'What the library does not do, what its examples assume, where the sensing examples stop, and how its two senses of quantum stay apart. Each row gives the file that shows it.';

const byDesign = boundaries.filter((b) => b.status === 'nongoal');
const inUse = boundaries.filter((b) => b.status === 'partial');
const cap = (s: string) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/boundaries/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Boundaries</p>
    <h1>You supply the physics; the library checks and decides.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The library computes every prediction from the response model you write, and it drives no
      hardware, runs no decoder and claims no code distance. Each row below gives the file that
      shows it.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="limits">
    <h2>{cap(numberWord(byDesign.length))} things it leaves out by design.</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      {byDesign.map((b) => (
        <li class="row" id={b.id}>
          <p class={`status status-${b.status}`}>{b.mark}</p>
          <div>
            <h3>{b.title}</h3>
            <p class="t">{b.text}</p>
            <p class="ev">Shown in <code>{b.evidence}</code></p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="in-use">
    <h2>{cap(numberWord(inUse.length))} limits you meet in use.</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      {inUse.map((b) => (
        <li class="row" id={b.id}>
          <p class={`status status-${b.status}`}>{b.mark}</p>
          <div>
            <h3>{b.title}</h3>
            <p class="t">{b.text}</p>
            <p class="ev">Shown in <code>{b.evidence}</code></p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="assumptions">
    <h2>The crosstalk example describes no device.</h2>
    <p>Its tables, shots and costs come from the example’s own files.</p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>The conditional tables are inputs</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>model.rs</code> states each candidate’s conditional tables and what each experiment
          does to them; the library computes every predicted read-out from those, and the plan and
          the verdict follow. The run shows the procedure reaching the correct candidate under
          those assumptions.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The observation is simulated</dt>
        <dd>
          The example draws its shots from the Born sampler at H1’s predicted read-out, then
          checks that the campaign finds H1; it exits nonzero if it does not. The campaign observes
          E1, the plan’s cheapest experiment, and stops there because H1 separates.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Cost is one arbitrary unit</dt>
        <dd>
          In the example’s constants, a targeted intervention costs 1, the passive read 1 and an
          echo 2, all in one unit. The costs come from those constants; no laboratory measured
          them.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The factors are diagonal</dt>
        <dd>
          Every candidate in the crosstalk example is a normalised, legal model by construction, so
          the normalisation and Markov checks reject none of them. The experiments alone separate
          the candidates.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>One candidate is not a circuit</dt>
        <dd>
          In the circuit version of the example, candidate H3 (the shared bath) stays a
          factorization. A circuit needs a bath node with two output wires. That node has a leg of
          256 dimensions, and the Markov union over its three legs is a 65536 × 65536 matrix with
          2<sup>32</sup> entries, above the crate’s cap of 2<sup>24</sup>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="sensing">
    <h2>The sensing results rest on simulation and placeholders.</h2>
    <p>
      Both examples behind the <a href="/sensing/">sensing section</a> simulate their observations,
      and a lab supplies the setup times, the tide, the leak and the dark background. Each row below
      gives one limit and the file that shows it.
    </p>
    <ul class="rows">
      {sensingBoundaries.map((b) => (
        <li class="row" id={b.id}>
          <p class={`status status-${b.status}`}>{b.mark}</p>
          <div>
            <h3>{b.title}</h3>
            <p class="t">{b.text}</p>
            <p class="ev">Shown in <code>{b.evidence}</code></p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <h3>What the sensing examples assume</h3>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>Each response belongs to one instrument</dt>
        <dd>
          Whether a field or a misalignment turns with the sensor head depends on the design.
          Each example’s <code>model.rs</code> states every response, and a lab confirms each one.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>One dominant cause per candidate</dt>
        <dd>
          A mixture of causes enters as a candidate of its own. The planner solves exactly up to
          seven candidates by default, and the gravimeter uses all seven.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Brightening one cloud reaches only the other’s signal</dt>
        <dd>
          The gradiometer’s E1 and E2 assume that pushing one cloud bright changes the other
          cloud’s detection signal and leaves its atoms alone. A lab confirms this.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The wavefront at two temperatures</dt>
        <dd>
          The gravimeter reads the wavefront at the operating 2 µK and at zero, and at no
          temperature between them.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="modalities">
    <h2>The default build compiles only the verifiable path.</h2>
    <p>
      The crate has two modalities. The Lean proofs attach to the verifiable one; the emergent one
      is a seam to a QPU sampler and needs the <code>qpu</code> feature.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Modality</th><th>What it is</th><th>Build</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Modality"><b>Verifiable</b></td>
            <td data-label="What it is">Deterministic simulated Choi–Jamiołkowski operators, checked at the freeze boundary; the Lean proofs attach here.</td>
            <td data-label="Build">Default</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Modality"><b>Emergent</b></td>
            <td data-label="What it is">A physical-QPU call lifted into the causal monad as a typed effect (<code>qpu_effect</code>), with an in-process <code>SimQpu</code> behind it.</td>
            <td data-label="Build"><code>qpu</code> feature</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The emergent seam defines types and drives no hardware, and it adds no network or async
      dependency. Its source is under
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/src/types/qpu`}><code>src/types/qpu/</code></a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .rows { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-5) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-h) + var(--space-4));
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .row { grid-template-columns: var(--w-label) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
  .status { margin: 0; }
  .row h3 { font-size: var(--t-h4); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); }
  .t { color: var(--fg-1); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); max-width: var(--measure); }
  .ev { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin: 0; }
  .ev code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .table-scroll table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .table-scroll th, .table-scroll td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .table-scroll thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
</style>
